Enhance dashboard with detailed analytics, logs, and user feedback integration.

Improve error handling and add more automation features.

Start development on additional modules (e.g., booking/scheduling).

Month 6:

Launch a broader beta release.

Implement robust API integrations with external systems.

Gather detailed user feedback for future enhancements.

Phase 3: Full Product Launch & Expansion (6+ months)
Post 6 Months:

Finalize additional modules for a full business operational workflow.

Optimize performance and scalability.

Roll out advanced features such as predictive analytics and personalized automation.

Expand to a wider market and continuously iterate based on user input.

5. User Stories / Use Cases
User Story 1: Auto Repair Shop Owner
Scenario: Joe, an auto repair shop owner, receives numerous service inquiry emails daily.

Process:

An email arrives in the system.

The Email MCP processes the email and the Conductor MCP determines if it’s a routine inquiry or requires a customized reply.

A draft reply is generated and sent to Joe’s dashboard for review.

Upon approval, the Email MCP sends out the reply.

Outcome: Joe saves time on email management and can focus more on customer service.

User Story 2: Car Wash Business Owner
Scenario: Sarah, who runs a car wash, wants to keep her website updated with seasonal promotions.

Process:

The Content Generation MCP is triggered on a schedule (or by Sarah’s manual input).

It pulls in relevant SEO trends and generates blog content along with promotional images.

The draft content appears on the dashboard for Sarah to review.

After approval, the content is published automatically.

Outcome: Sarah’s website stays current and engaging, driving more customers to her business.

User Story 3: Cleaning Service Business Owner
Scenario: Michael manages a cleaning service and needs to handle both service inquiries and schedule appointments.

Process:

A customer inquiry about cleaning services arrives via email.

The Conductor MCP routes the inquiry to both the Email MCP (for an initial response) and the Scheduling MCP (to propose available time slots).

Michael receives a consolidated update on his dashboard, reviews the suggestions, and confirms the appointment.

Outcome: Michael’s operational efficiency increases as the system automates routine scheduling and communication tasks.

